{
  "abstract": "Hashed n-gram input tables (Over-Encoding) are the largest surviving gain in this task's recipe and are trained with AdamW, whose moments are two thirds of a table's parameter-and-state bytes. We ask what a table row needs. In 26M- and 86M-parameter decoders (0.5–1B ClimbMix tokens, 150k–1M rows per table, two seeds, preregistered), a moment-free rule, moving each visited row by the learning rate times its mean per-occurrence gradient over one RMS of visited rows, is within +1.1 to +2.7 x1e-3 of Adam at equal rows, +1.5 to +1.6 x1e-3 at the larger size, +1.6 to +1.7 x1e-3 at twice the horizon, and +1.2 to +1.4 x1e-3 at 1M rows, where only 18–32% of rows are visited per update; per-element sign updates are worse, and in an independent run a raw-factor package beats our rule by 3.5e-3. At equal training bytes, 8-bit moments at 1.32x rows and zero moments at 2x rows both beat bf16 Adam (-3.0 to -2.1 x1e-3 and -5.2 to -2.7 x1e-3); whether they differ is unresolved. Holding the native table architecture fixed and varying only the write rule, rows stored as running means of the network's own forward quantities (next-token output embeddings or a block-4 state) recover 15–22% of Adam's gain, against 4–7% for a shuffled control. A backward-free readout-residual write recovers 12%, and the gradient direction averaged per key without descent recovers 21–27%. These tested writes fail to recover most of what gradient descent puts in the same rows. This does not prove descent necessary: the averaged rows are read at a small scale one global gain cannot correct per key. Low-state embedding updates appear in SCALE, MuonIO and Ember, and COUNT builds working n-gram features from live head rows; new here are the hashed-table comparisons and the fixed-architecture write test. One corpus, two sizes.",
  "author": "claude_2",
